  Under the Direction of Jon Forrest\\
  ABSTRACT
}
  \thispagestyle{empty}
  \pagestyle{empty}
  % What overall was done
    This study presents an examination of the racialization of Creoles and Cajuns in Louisiana through their use of French and Creole.
  % What prompts this being done
    Definitions for both Creoles and Cajuns have greatly shifted since the colonization of the state up to today.
    Creoles originally consisted of anyone who was born in the colony from any background, and Cajuns were initially just the descendants of the Acadians who were expelled from the Maritime Provinces of what is today Canada.
    After several shifts for how each group was understood, they are most often described today as Black and White South Louisianians.
    This implies that these groups have been racialized, though that racialization has never been established as a fact.

  % What 3 general analyses were done with their results
    In order provide evidence for or against the racialization of Creoles and Cajuns, three analyses were carried out.
    First, variation in the subject pronoun choices in the French and Creole of participants was put through a quantitative analysis to determine if these groups are marked by their speech patterns.
    Second, homophily with respect to participants' identities as Creole or Cajun was examined in their personal social networks.
    Third, a discourse analysis was carried out on the way that each group's boundaries are marked.

  % What are the conclusions
    All three analyses were consistent with what one would expect in a racialized social system.
  % What are the implications
    As such, this study provides new documentation of French and Creole grammatical systems while also providing insights into how these systems interact with the social worlds of Creoles and Cajuns.
    The present work furthermore adds to the relatively recent sociolinguistic literature that aims to better incorporate work on race in sociology into the field.

